  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,5),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =y+4 \\\\ 25 & =y+4 \\\\ y & =25-4 \\\\ y & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $21.$",
